Subject: [PATCH] feat(kimi_k3): add moonshotai/Kimi-K3

Kimi K3 is 93 layers, 69 Kimi Delta Attention and 24 gated MLA with NoPE,
hidden 7168, 896 routed experts top-16 plus 2 shared. 2.8T parameters, 104B
active. sglang-jax has kimi_linear.py for Kimi-Linear-48B-A3B, which shares the
KDA recurrence and the two-pool split but nothing above them.

Four architecture changes over Kimi-Linear-48B, and the checkpoint is mixed
precision.

1. hidden_act is "situ": beta * tanh(g / beta) * sigmoid(g) times a
   linear_beta-bounded up branch. It goes in layers/activation.py, because both
   the dense MLPs and the MoE experts run it, and EPMoE takes it as
   activation="situ". The dense and shared-expert MLPs subclass
   kimi_linear.KimiMLP, which builds the same three projections.

2. Latent MoE. routed_expert_hidden_size is 3584, half the residual width. The
   router scores the full 7168 stream, routed_expert_down_proj enters the
   latent, the experts run 3584 -> 3072 -> 3584, routed_expert_norm normalizes
   and routed_expert_up_proj returns. The shared experts stay at full width.
   Both latent projections are row parallel, so the 49.0 MiB each one weighs
   splits across the tensor axis instead of landing whole on every chip.

3. attn_res_block_size is 12. The residual stream is a learned softmax mixture
   over the running prefix sum and one snapshot per 12-layer block, not a
   single additive accumulator. attn_res_mix holds it.

4. use_full_rank_gate puts one 7168 x 12288 g_proj in each KDA layer where
   Kimi-Linear-48B factors the output gate through a rank-128 pair.

The KDA layers keep Kimi-Linear's one log decay per head. The published
checkpoint ships language_model.model.layers.N.self_attn.A_log as a flat
float32 [128], which is head_dim, for 96 heads. In all 69 KDA layers the first
96 values hold the decays and the last 32 hold zeros. The reference
modeling_kimi_linear.py declares A_log as torch.empty(num_heads), FLA's KDA
kernels read it at A_log + i_h, and vLLM and SGLang narrow it to its first
num_heads entries. WeightMapping gains narrow, which keeps the first entries
along one axis before any reshape. The A_log mapping keeps 96 and holds them at
[1, 1, 96, 1], the layout kimi_linear.py holds, so the KDA backend and kernels
run unchanged. The recurrence, the three short convolutions, dt_bias, b_proj,
the gate_lower_bound of -5.0 and o_norm transfer unchanged too.

The MLA layers add a sigmoid output gate, which fits the _pre_o_proj hook
DeepseekV3Attention already exposes.

quantization_config.ignore exempts attention, shared experts, the dense layer
and lm_head, so those load BF16 through WeightLoader. Only the routed experts
ship MXFP4: weight_packed uint8 with two E2M1 codes per byte, weight_scale
uint8 with one E8M0 exponent per 32 elements. utils/quantization/mxfp4.py
dequantizes and KimiK3ForCausalLM._load_routed_experts drives it through
jax.make_array_from_callback, so a host only ever holds the shard it owns.
read_mxfp4_block cuts both edges of that shard off the file before it decodes,
so a host that owns a thirty-second of a matrix reads a thirty-second of it.
_load_routed_experts reads the expert-location metadata and indexes the
checkpoint by logical expert, which is what create_moe_weights_mapping does for
models whose experts load through WeightLoader.

The routed experts stay MXFP4 in HBM by default. EPMoE takes mxfp4_resident:
wi_0, wi_1 and wo then hold the packed E2M1 codes as uint8 [E, in / 2, out],
and three more parameters hold the E8M0 scales as uint8 [E, in / 32, out], on
the BF16 stacks' expert and tensor sharding. wo's scales stay whole on the
tensor axis, because a tensor shard of wo's packed axis needn't end on a
32-element group. The load callback cuts each device's rectangle off the file
and decodes nothing. _gmm_compute decodes each stack to the serving dtype with
mxfp4.dequantize_mxfp4_jax just before its gmm, inside the shard_map, so each
device decodes its own experts and one decoded stack is live at a time. An
optimization barrier ties each decode, the packed codes and the E8M0 scales
both, to the activation its gmm reads. The scales need it too. Their expansion
reads nothing but the scale parameter, and with only the codes barriered XLA
ran every layer's expansion at the start of the program and held each one
until its layer's gmm: a per-element gather index and an f32 factor, each the
size of the decoded stack, 1,176 MiB a chip at --ep-size 32. The first extend
precompile on the v5p-64 then asked for 196.32 GB of HLO temporaries a chip.
Rows that start on a 32-element group now read one E8M0 factor per group and
broadcast it, so the lookup is 1/32 of the stack.
A TPU cross-compile of the full published config for a 32-chip v5p:2x4x4 at
the check's extend shape asks for 201.83 GB of temporaries before and 5.63 GiB
after, beside 48.35 GiB of weights and 28.25 GiB of pools.
dequantize_mxfp4_jax makes the float32 products dequantize_mxfp4 makes, so the
served logits and every captured layer match the decode-at-load path bit for
bit. At --tp-size 32 --ep-size 32 the weights take 48.35 GiB a chip, so Kimi
K3 fits one v5p-64 at --dp-size 1, where capture works.
--json-model-override-args '{"mxfp4_resident_experts": false}' decodes at load
instead, and a launch quantization config or --moe-dp-size above 1 does so by
default.

The router hands the expert-location metadata to TopK, the call deepseek_v3
makes, and with --ep-dispatch-algorithm set TopK rewrites logical expert ids
into physical ones in eplb/expert_location.py. On the engine's Explicit mesh the
ids arrive sharded over the batch and the maps arrive unsharded, so the stock
gathers can't work out where their output lives, and the startup precompile
raises, for the static map and the dynamic one alike. Each gather now takes the
ids' sharding, and the dynamic path's random offsets reshard to match.
gpt-oss-model.patch carries the same hunk.

EPMoE builds its stacks under use_abstract_mesh, so after the loader's
nnx.eval_shape each one carries a NamedSharding over an AbstractMesh, which
names no devices. expert_sharding moves the spec onto EPMoE.moe_mesh, the
concrete mesh with the same axis names, before make_array_from_callback runs.
The stack comes out in the serving dtype, and every MXFP4 value is exact in
BF16. A dummy load reads no file: load_weights fills the stacks with zeros on
that mesh before the loader's dummy pass, which would lay each stack whole on
every chip, and WeightLoader.checkpoint_index answers an empty index.

GateLogit keeps e_score_correction_bias in F32, the dtype the checkpoint ships,
because top-16 picks on score plus bias. lm_head loads through
ParallelLMHead.weight_mapping, so it takes the data-and-tensor split the head
declares and any vocab padding. EPMoE takes the launch's QuantizationConfig and
nothing else; the checkpoint's own quantization_config is a compressed-tensors
dict EPMoE can't read. ModelConfig marks any --quantization-config-path static
on this checkpoint, because that dict says compressed-tensors, and nothing here
loads a statically quantized checkpoint, so KimiK3ForCausalLM refuses one
before the loader swaps its linears.

KimiK3Model starts the block stash as a zero-width slice of the embedding, so
it carries the stream's sharding. A bare jnp.zeros is replicated, and under the
engine's Explicit mesh layer 0's concatenate refuses it.

WeightLoader gains an externally_loaded predicate so coverage validation counts
keys the model accounts for as handled and still catches a key nobody reads.
Kimi K3 names three groups: the MXFP4 routed experts, the 168 vision_tower.*
and mm_projector.* tensors in the last two shards that the language tower
serves nothing for, and every decoder layer at or past num_hidden_layers, which
--model-layer-nums cuts and WeightLoader skips only under a bare model.layers.
prefix. load_weights turns the validation on.

Three engine fixes let the model serve through NativeAttention, with
--attention-backend native. HybridLinearKVPool forwards set_kv_buffer_legacy,
which NativeAttention writes through off TPU. DeepseekV3Attention._forward_mha
reads V's width off the attention output, because NativeAttention hands V back
at the KV pool's 128-aligned width, 256 for K3's 192-wide head. And under
--dp-size above 1 each data rank's cache indices address its own slice of the
pool. NativeAttention attends one rank at a time, on TPU and off it. Off TPU it
also shifts the write's indices onto the rank's slice, because the legacy write
takes global rows. On TPU the pool's sharded kernel writes each rank's rows
into that rank's shard as they come.

The default --attention-backend fa doesn't serve off TPU. A CPU run swaps in
NativeAttention for fa, but server_args.attention_backend stays fa, and
ModelRunner reads it to pick the absorbed MLA path and the latent
MLATokenToKVPool, which only the MLA Pallas kernel writes. So
HybridLinearKVPool.set_kv_buffer_legacy refuses an inner pool with no legacy
write, by name, and points at --attention-backend native.

configs/kimi_linear.py gains the K3 fields and unwraps text_config, so
get_kimi_linear_config answers for both model_type kimi_linear and
model_type kimi_k3. Kimi-Linear-48B leaves every new field at its default.

Two pools, as in kimi_linear.py: the 69 KDA layers hold state in
RecurrentStatePool and the 24 MLA layers hold a KV cache in HybridLinearKVPool.
configs/kimi_linear.py also gains KimiK3Config for model_type kimi_k3. The
published config.json nests every language-model field under text_config and
keeps architectures, model_type and the token ids at the top, and AutoConfig
hands the outer object to the model class. KimiK3Config subclasses
KimiLinearConfig and copies the text fields onto itself, so hidden_size,
linear_attn_config and is_kda_layer all read the language model. Top-level keys
win the merge, which keeps architectures at KimiK3ForConditionalGeneration.
Its text_config is the same object, so the model, ModelConfig.hf_text_config
and the state pools read one set of fields. --json-model-override-args then
reaches all three, at the top level or nested under text_config.
hf_transformers_utils registers it, so AutoConfig accepts the checkpoint.
KimiK3ForCausalLM keeps the object the loader hands over, because
ModelRunner.load_model writes ep_size, moe_dp_size, use_absorbed_mla and the
rest onto it.

The checkpoint lists KimiK3ForConditionalGeneration in its top-level
architectures and get_model_architecture resolves that list against the
registry, so EntryClass carries that name beside KimiK3ForCausalLM. The
language tower is the whole of what this file serves, so the two names run the
same code. Neither joins InModelMultimodalContract, which is what marks a model
multimodal, so a text request runs untouched.
